00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:51.070
We at T-Mobile are on
a journey to change

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:54.100
the wireless experience
of our customers.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.460
In today's digital age of
mobile-ware, artificial

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:00.880
intelligence, machine
learning, internet of things,

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.410
changing the customer
experience is

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:05.650
impossible without leveraging
the power of the APIs

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:08.510
across the digital value chain.

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:11.920
Hence, let's reflect back
on our API journey so far.

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:18.640
We started with Apigee API
Gateway back in 2015 with two

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.279
dedicated API teams
developing APIs

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:25.600
to expose legacy services for
a new radically simplified

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.860
frontline application.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:31.520
In 2017, to bring some
cohesiveness among the growing

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.870
API teams, Proxy Gen, a
homegrown API development tool,

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:37.220
was launched.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:41.360
It would take the API contract
or the [INAUDIBLE] as an input

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.270
and generate the Apigee
API proxy bundle,

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:48.250
check the code into the source
code management repository,

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:51.760
launch the CI/CD pipelines,
and deploy the Apigee proxy

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:53.950
into Apigee Edge.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:56.320
It was an instant hit
with the API developer

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:59.110
community at T-Mobile.

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:02.530
API Center for Enablement
was launched in 2018

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:06.130
to address the growing
challenges with the API program

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:08.980
that was gradually emerging.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.950
Same year, we launched a suite
of API first development tools

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:16.100
to enable the domain teams
to self-serve themselves

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:20.050
for any need on the
Apigee API platform.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:24.450
And here we are in 2020 with
80-plus business domain teams

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:27.240
developing APIs within T-Mobile.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.750
Together, they have produced
1,300-plus API proxies,

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:35.360
resulting in over 9 billion
transactions per month.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.080
Let's look at some of the API
operational challenges that

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.120
come with this kind of growth.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.250
Here are some of the
challenges we continually

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:46.670
face every day in the
API operation space.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:49.280
And some of these might
resonate with your company's API

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:51.620
operations as well.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:55.040
Securing and protecting APIs
from external and internal

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:59.330
bad actors, bot traffic,
denial-of-service attacks,

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:03.190
broken authentication,
and so on and so forth,

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:06.440
and trying to continually
maintain a secure posture

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:11.590
to have a seamless digital
experience for our customers.

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:15.820
Observability into unexpected
and unforeseen API runtime

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:18.580
issues and contextual
information about those

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:21.790
issues is a key challenge
to improve the API

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:24.590
availability and performance.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:28.880
API platform scalability to
handle the sudden traffic burst

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:31.700
during our Un-Carrier
move announcements,

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:34.700
like our latest Un-Carrier
move, Scam Shield,

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:37.120
and promotional events,
like Black Friday holiday

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:39.650
season, et cetera.

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:42.050
On a similar note,
capacity planning

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:44.990
to support new innovative
growth that we foresee

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:47.540
beyond wireless, like
our banking service,

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:52.560
T-Mobile Money, our TV
service, T-Vision, and others.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:56.250
And then streamlining the API
consumer and partner onboarding

00:06:56.250 --> 00:07:00.780
process to access T-Mobile APIs
to improve the API consumer

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.140
and partner experience
and agility.

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:06.810
AI and ML have
definitely helped us

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:08.700
in addressing some
of these challenges.

00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:15.360
AI and ML features, like API
monitoring and Apigee Sense,

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:19.560
have definitely empowered
our everyday API operations

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.890
by covering our blind side
for unforeseen API traffic

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:26.470
and performance issues, which
could go undetected and cause

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:29.460
severe API
availability incidents

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:32.910
and impact the
business negatively.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:35.280
Anomaly detection
and API operations

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.550
by applying AI and
ML models is aligned

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:41.370
with T-Mobile's
observability objectives,

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:44.970
like proactively monitoring
the systems and APIs,

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:47.610
detecting issues prior
to any negative impact

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:51.360
on the business, and developing
automated capabilities where

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.980
possible.

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:57.820
Alerting and notification
combined with anomaly detection

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:00.730
or real-time traffic
and performance issues

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:03.370
has improved our
team's productivity

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:07.930
and significantly reduced the
MTTD, mean time to diagnose,

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:10.720
API incidents.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:12.670
With the added
suggested operator

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:15.850
controls in the form of
predetermined resolution

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:21.580
labels or any issues has notably
reduced the MTTR, mean time

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:26.320
to resolution, of
any API incident.

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:30.220
Let's look at a demo on how AI
and ML have empowered our API

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:33.309
operations at T-Mobile.

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:36.580
Let's check out some of the
Apigee's built-in AI and ML

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:40.059
features in real time and how
these features are assisting

00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:43.659
in keeping T-Mobile APIs
available and performant

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:45.580
for a seamless
digital experience

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:47.920
of T-Mobile customers.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:49.600
When you log into
Apigee Edge, this

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:52.600
is how the landing
page looks like.

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:56.140
You can get to all the
observability related features

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:57.280
under the Analyze tab.

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:05.890
As a DevOps engineer, I visit
the API Monitoring Overview

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:09.640
dashboard quite frequently.

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:12.550
This dashboard provides
me with a quick summary

00:09:12.550 --> 00:09:16.090
about the runtime telemetry of
all the API proxies deployed

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:19.380
in the production environment.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.630
For the purposes
of this demo, we

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:24.930
will be using one of our
development environments,

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:30.540
wherein hopefully you will
not see a lot of broken stuff.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:32.800
As you can see in
this dashboard,

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:38.670
we get a summary information
about a given API environment,

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:44.160
total traffic and transactions
per second, overall average

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:48.600
error rate percentage,
top API proxy response

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:54.760
P99 latency value, and total
number of recent alerts.

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:59.410
It also provides top three
API proxies with highest value

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:03.300
in each metric category.

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:05.400
So this dashboard
provides a DevOps

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:10.050
engineer a pretty good overview
of any potential runtime issues

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:13.380
with any API proxy.

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.930
Now let's move on
to the Events tab

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:20.070
to check if there have been
any recent alerts triggered

00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:23.700
or any new anomalies detected.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:26.400
Looks like we will have to
go a little bit back in time

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:28.592
to see any alerts and anomaly.

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:33.310
Here you go.

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:37.980
So the Alert tab shows all
the recent triggered alerts

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:42.960
based on the predefined
conditions set by us.

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:46.260
The Anomaly tab
shows all the events

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:49.230
that deviate from the
normal behavioral pattern

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:53.970
at the Apigee region,
organization, and environment

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:56.100
level.

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:59.400
These deviations, as
well as the severity,

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:03.720
is detected by applying the
built-in AI and ML models

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:07.690
to the historical API data.

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:11.980
The cool thing about the anomaly
is the contextual information

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:16.090
it provides with respect
to environment name, API

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:19.750
proxy name, target,
fault code region,

00:11:19.750 --> 00:11:22.990
and the time of the trigger.

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:26.560
For a DevOps engineer,
this contextual information

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:31.060
about an anomaly is really
critical and important,

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:36.070
as it significantly reduces
the overall MTTD, mean time

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:40.390
to diagnose, and MTTR,
mean time to resolution,

00:11:40.390 --> 00:11:43.690
for any unexpected
and unforeseen issues

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:46.870
within our APIs which
might be in our blind side.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:51.310
And the amazing thing
about this feature

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:57.140
is that we do not have to do
anything to set all this up.

00:11:57.140 --> 00:12:01.660
It comes by default
and is built in.

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:04.990
So based on this
contextual information,

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:07.450
if a DevOps engineer
decides to dive

00:12:07.450 --> 00:12:10.690
in deeper into the
anomaly, he or she

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:14.120
can drill down into
the anomaly, and it

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:19.260
will take them into the
Investigate page or the tab.

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:21.470
So let's investigate
one of the anomalies.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:29.410
Just looking at the
anomaly details on the top,

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:31.660
looks like this
anomaly was detected

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:36.880
due to unreasonably high number
of 504 response code in the API

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:43.280
flow, which started at
around 3:17 in the afternoon.

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:47.180
Let's look at why the API
is returning the 504 error

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:49.290
response to the clients.

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:52.910
Now, I have various ways
and different dimensions

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:55.500
that I can find the root
cause about this issue.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:59.000
Let's try to determine the
fourth source in the API flow

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:02.000
by looking at
different dimensions.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:04.400
I would want to look
at the false source

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:05.760
at this point in time.

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:09.530
So I'll go select that
dimension from the dropdown.

00:13:09.530 --> 00:13:13.100
And as we can see
in the API flow,

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:15.890
it is pretty obvious
that the target endpoint

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:18.040
is the problematic area.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:23.050
So let's dig in further by
clicking on the target cell.

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:25.375
Here, we can see
the suspected cause

00:13:25.375 --> 00:13:28.090
determined by the ML model.

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:31.600
This can be attributed to
the fault code, which clearly

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:33.850
states that the error
response code is being

00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:36.070
written by the backend service.

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:39.550
Also, there are details for the
impacted clients, API clients,

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:43.780
and the target endpoints
that this API is dependent

00:13:43.780 --> 00:13:47.930
upon and is at fault
for this anomaly.

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:51.500
Now, if we want to look at any
specific transactional level

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:54.650
information, we can do so
by going into View Logs.

00:14:01.370 --> 00:14:06.170
This view provides us
into specific details

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:11.000
about each transaction, like the
request URI, which is basically

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:13.610
the URI being invoked
by the API client

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:16.340
along with the request
message ID, what

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:19.340
is the status code,
response time, [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:19.340 --> 00:14:25.790
source, and other details about
the client and the transaction.

00:14:25.790 --> 00:14:28.870
Next, I can do additional
things with this anomaly,

00:14:28.870 --> 00:14:32.820
like creating an alert
based off this anomaly.

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:36.460
So let's quickly create an
alert based off this anomaly.

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:39.490
We'll have to hit this
button, Create Alert Tab,

00:14:39.490 --> 00:14:45.200
and we'll have to come up with
an alert name of our choice,

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:49.150
provide the description
to it, what type of alert.

00:14:49.150 --> 00:14:51.430
If we want to base this
alert based off this anomaly,

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:53.710
we can leave it at anomaly.

00:14:53.710 --> 00:14:56.830
And then alter the
condition if we choose to

00:14:56.830 --> 00:15:01.390
or let it remain the same
as detected by the anomaly.

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:06.100
And we have to select the alert
notification type we want,

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:10.840
like email notification or Slack
or a PagerDuty notification.

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:12.820
Once you have filled
out this form,

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:17.170
we just have to save this, and
we have a pre-configured alert

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:21.520
for a given anomaly condition
for future proactive

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:22.930
notifications.

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:26.860
So let me share some
sample alert notifications.

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:33.430
Here is one email
alert notification

00:15:33.430 --> 00:15:35.410
and how it looks like.

00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:38.140
It provides us information
like the timestamp

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:44.500
of the trigger, org name, alert
name, summary of the alert,

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:46.000
and details
regarding the alerts,

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:50.770
like the condition, trigger
value, proxy region, et cetera.

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:54.250
And it also provides embedded
links to directly view

00:15:54.250 --> 00:15:55.780
the event in Apigee Edge.

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:03.180
This is how a sample Slack
notification looks like.

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:06.150
It provides us the
name of the alert,

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:09.570
organization name,
special violation details,

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:15.450
like the target latency,
proxy name, region,

00:16:15.450 --> 00:16:19.590
condition information,
so on and so forth.

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:26.310
It also provides us web links
as well to view the anomaly

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:29.100
or the alert details
directly into the Events

00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:30.410
tab of Apigee Edge.

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:37.240
So let's go back.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:39.860
Now, I can do further
things, like I

00:16:39.860 --> 00:16:43.280
can create a report based
off this anomaly to look

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:47.720
for any additional dimensions
for further analysis,

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:52.980
or for an RCA, or for
management reporting needs.

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:56.090
We are really impressed by
the API monitoring feature,

00:16:56.090 --> 00:16:59.540
as it has helped mitigate
latency and network

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:04.670
issues in the past, resulting
in increased API availability,

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:08.705
along with improved
performance of our APIs.

00:17:08.705 --> 00:17:10.790
Next, let's see
how collections can

00:17:10.790 --> 00:17:13.400
be really useful in
making the most out

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:15.234
of these AI and ML features.

00:17:19.490 --> 00:17:23.800
Collections are group of API
proxies, targets, developer

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:28.190
apps based on the organization
choice of dimension.

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:32.800
We at T-Mobile have collections
based off business domain APIs,

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:35.870
which helps us in setting
up appropriate alert

00:17:35.870 --> 00:17:38.930
configurations and
threshold values

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:42.320
for the same group of APIs.

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:44.420
Next, I want to
move on to security.

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:49.870
Security is one of
the single biggest

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:52.780
challenge that most of
the digital enterprises

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:54.530
are trying to solve.

00:17:54.530 --> 00:17:58.540
And it never gets easier, with
ever-evolving newer threats

00:17:58.540 --> 00:18:01.270
surfacing every day.

00:18:01.270 --> 00:18:04.150
In the Security Reporting
Overview dashboard,

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:08.230
we have information with respect
to northbound API traffic

00:18:08.230 --> 00:18:12.370
for all the given
environments, and it

00:18:12.370 --> 00:18:15.670
provides further information
regarding the API proxies,

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:19.610
taking active traffic out
of the total API deployed,

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:21.690
total number of [INAUDIBLE]
deployed in a given

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:24.880
environment, total
traffic, and the percent

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:27.380
exchanged in traffic.

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:32.320
It also provides the API client
traffic composition by region.

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:35.350
Since this is one of our
development environment,

00:18:35.350 --> 00:18:40.150
and most of our developers are
based in the US West Coast,

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:42.250
hence, we can see the
corresponding traffic

00:18:42.250 --> 00:18:46.960
competition being higher
in the US West region.

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:50.050
Let's move on to the
Runtime dashboard, which

00:18:50.050 --> 00:18:52.900
provides us more
specific and detailed

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:56.860
runtime information
about the API proxies.

00:18:56.860 --> 00:18:59.830
It provides information
about the API proxy traffic,

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:02.670
percentage change in
traffic, and percentage

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:06.700
of traffic on non-HTTPS
or non-secured code,

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:08.740
and the total number
of API clients

00:19:08.740 --> 00:19:11.530
in working or
accessing this API.

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:16.180
And if I want to look for
the details into API proxy,

00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:19.270
it provides us a
visualized graph

00:19:19.270 --> 00:19:24.610
for the HTTPS and non-HTTPS
API access protocols.

00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:26.840
And then it also provides
further data points

00:19:26.840 --> 00:19:28.900
with respect to
developer apps, which

00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:31.060
will host and target
endpoints that this API

00:19:31.060 --> 00:19:35.180
proxy is dependent on.

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:39.580
Let's move on to the
configuration of the API

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:42.290
proxies.

00:19:42.290 --> 00:19:43.900
This particular
dashboard provides

00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:47.530
us insight into the API
proxy's security configuration

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:53.280
and policy
composition, as well as

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:56.040
providing information about
the virtual host definitions

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:57.190
as well.

00:19:57.190 --> 00:19:59.310
So different
categories of policies

00:19:59.310 --> 00:20:01.350
include the traffic
management, security,

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:05.940
and extension policies, as
well as reusable piece of code

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:09.380
in the form of shared flows.

00:20:09.380 --> 00:20:13.380
So virtual host is a
definition in API proxy

00:20:13.380 --> 00:20:17.380
which determines how
the API clients--

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:19.210
or on which protocol
the API clients

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:21.250
are going to access the APIs.

00:20:21.250 --> 00:20:24.970
If we want to dig deeper
into specific security policy

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:28.270
details that are
part of API proxy,

00:20:28.270 --> 00:20:30.400
we can click on
API Proxy, and we

00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:35.650
can see specific policy names
that are part of this given API

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:37.180
proxy.

00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:42.040
And then if there are some
security policy standards that

00:20:42.040 --> 00:20:44.530
have been established
by our API program,

00:20:44.530 --> 00:20:48.460
we can evaluate each and every
proxy against those standards

00:20:48.460 --> 00:20:53.170
to see whether these APIs
are in compliance or not.

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:56.720
So this dashboard is very
helpful in a quick glance

00:20:56.720 --> 00:21:02.270
on the security posture of our
runtime API configurations.

00:21:02.270 --> 00:21:05.740
So all of this data that is
visualized on this dashboard

00:21:05.740 --> 00:21:11.380
is also available via APIs
for the security organization

00:21:11.380 --> 00:21:14.380
to integrate this data with the
custom dashboards and reporting

00:21:14.380 --> 00:21:16.580
tools.

00:21:16.580 --> 00:21:20.360
Finally, we're going to
talk about user activity.

00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:24.230
As a User Activity dashboard
has individual user identifying

00:21:24.230 --> 00:21:27.380
information, which
I cannot display,

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:31.040
therefore I have screenshots
for the User Activity Details

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:34.600
dashboard.

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:37.120
User Activity dashboard
provides insight

00:21:37.120 --> 00:21:40.050
into the user access and
their access behavior

00:21:40.050 --> 00:21:48.860
at on the Apigee Edge UI
or via the Management APIs,

00:21:48.860 --> 00:21:50.570
as well as change
in user activity

00:21:50.570 --> 00:21:53.220
over the period of time
and what type of data

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:55.200
they are accessing
and percentage

00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:59.720
of potentially sensitive
operations they have performed.

00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:03.630
If we identify any suspicious
activity by a user,

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:08.740
we can drill down further on
all the activities and actions

00:22:08.740 --> 00:22:12.220
performed by a particular user,
like the resources they have

00:22:12.220 --> 00:22:15.730
accessed and what
action they have taken

00:22:15.730 --> 00:22:18.085
on those particular resources.

00:22:18.085 --> 00:22:21.460
To recap, security
reporting feature

00:22:21.460 --> 00:22:24.310
provides us with relevant
data points and matrix

00:22:24.310 --> 00:22:27.460
to evaluate whether API
proxies are adhering

00:22:27.460 --> 00:22:31.090
to security policies and
configuration requirements set

00:22:31.090 --> 00:22:35.670
forth by the organization's
digital security team.

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:39.690
Lastly, realizing the value
of these productive AI and ML

00:22:39.690 --> 00:22:43.020
features is the key to have
highly available and performant

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:46.020
APIs for any API program.

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:49.490
Next, let's review some real
world business outcomes,

00:22:49.490 --> 00:22:52.950
wherein these features were
immensely valuable for our API

00:22:52.950 --> 00:22:55.110
DevOps and [INAUDIBLE] Team.

00:22:55.110 --> 00:22:58.560
Bot traffic is estimated to
be 40% of overall internet

00:22:58.560 --> 00:22:59.400
traffic.

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:03.500
And out of those, 20%
is bad bot traffic.

00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:06.260
Speaking of bad bot traffic,
one of the anomalies

00:23:06.260 --> 00:23:09.620
we had was certain surge
in traffic to our auth

00:23:09.620 --> 00:23:14.630
API during off-business
hours, which is odd.

00:23:14.630 --> 00:23:16.850
Upon diagnosing the
issue, we found out

00:23:16.850 --> 00:23:18.800
that some bad
actors overseas were

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:21.470
trying to harvest the
access tokens from the auth

00:23:21.470 --> 00:23:25.910
API using bots,
specifically bad bots.

00:23:25.910 --> 00:23:28.310
As this was a major
security incident,

00:23:28.310 --> 00:23:30.680
we moved fast to
remedy the issue.

00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:33.800
Coupled with Apigee Sense
and other [INAUDIBLE] tools,

00:23:33.800 --> 00:23:35.810
we were able to
successfully block

00:23:35.810 --> 00:23:39.740
the bot traffic very quickly.

00:23:39.740 --> 00:23:43.090
Next, network latencies can
cause substantial degradation

00:23:43.090 --> 00:23:46.970
in service and negatively
impact the SLAs.

00:23:46.970 --> 00:23:49.430
Another anomaly detected
was network latency

00:23:49.430 --> 00:23:51.950
in API response times.

00:23:51.950 --> 00:23:54.950
API transactions were making
coast-to-coast round trip

00:23:54.950 --> 00:23:57.960
to our East Coast data center,
even though the traffic

00:23:57.960 --> 00:24:02.450
originated in US West region,
adding substantial latency

00:24:02.450 --> 00:24:05.330
and negatively impacting
our customers' digital web

00:24:05.330 --> 00:24:07.540
experience.

00:24:07.540 --> 00:24:10.720
Upon diagnosing the issue,
the major cloud provider

00:24:10.720 --> 00:24:14.830
unconsciously made changes to
their networking routing logic.

00:24:14.830 --> 00:24:17.010
To elevate the
issue [INAUDIBLE],,

00:24:17.010 --> 00:24:20.440
we made a few routing logic
changes in our API flow.

00:24:20.440 --> 00:24:22.090
But ultimately,
the cloud provider

00:24:22.090 --> 00:24:27.450
was able to permanently fix the
network issue on their side.

00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:30.180
New product launches
and new promotions,

00:24:30.180 --> 00:24:33.750
like our latest Scam Shield
Uncarrier move are big events

00:24:33.750 --> 00:24:37.900
and can bring huge surge
in traffic to our websites.

00:24:37.900 --> 00:24:40.890
Next anomaly detected was a
sudden burst in API traffic

00:24:40.890 --> 00:24:44.770
for T-Vision APIs when
they were launched.

00:24:44.770 --> 00:24:49.630
We expected a surge but not
the extent of 700% increase.

00:24:49.630 --> 00:24:52.840
Since this was one of
the SaS environments,

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:54.640
Apigee Support
Team at Google was

00:24:54.640 --> 00:24:57.370
contacted to check up on
auto-scale capabilities

00:24:57.370 --> 00:25:02.350
of the Apigee runtime competence
to handle the additional load.

00:25:02.350 --> 00:25:04.300
All of these incidents
were detected

00:25:04.300 --> 00:25:08.620
by API monitoring, which
is a testament to AI and ML

00:25:08.620 --> 00:25:12.430
capabilities for automated
monitoring and alerting,

00:25:12.430 --> 00:25:14.140
along with the
ability to dig deeper

00:25:14.140 --> 00:25:18.430
into an anomaly or incident in
real time to diagnose the issue

00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:21.200
and find resolution for it.

00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:24.500
To conclude, AI and
ML have significantly

00:25:24.500 --> 00:25:27.830
transformed the business
of operating APIs,

00:25:27.830 --> 00:25:31.850
and in near future, using
advanced predictive analytics,

00:25:31.850 --> 00:25:34.310
the ability to predict
incidents before they

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:37.600
impact customer and
probably self-resolve

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:40.550
will elevate it
to the next level.

00:25:40.550 --> 00:25:43.092
Thank you, and
back to you, Manoj.

